Change request 13: Let the owner skip a required interview stage if they write why.
Seeded problem (the answer key): {"problem": "conflict", "type": "other:required-stages", "against": "only optional interview stages may be skipped"}
Elements it is bound to: ["authoring/action:skip_stage"]; note: the rule is the kernel's (only optional interview stages may be skipped), not an element; bound to skip_stage, the action the change would loosen (the researcher chose option A over unbindable, 2026-11-06)

=== A ===
The edit: --- a/authoring/authoring.domain.yaml
+++ b/authoring/authoring.domain.yaml
@@ -779,7 +779,8 @@
       ref: records:docs/adr/0037-switch-over-to-the-generated-self-domains.md#L8-L25
     status: confirmed
   skip_stage:
-    description: Say an optional stage has nothing, in the owner's words.
+    description: Say a stage, optional or required, has nothing for this domain, in
+      the owner's words. A required stage can be skipped only with a written reason.
     actor:
     - owner
     target: Draft
@@ -789,6 +790,11 @@
         type: string
       reason:
         type: string
+    preconditions:
+    - id: skip-needs-a-reason
+      check: params.reason != ''
+      reason: A stage is skipped only with the owner's written reason why it has nothing.
+      status: proposed
     effects:
     - call: skip_stage
       with:

A.1.0. {"problem": "The new precondition `params.reason != ''` applies to every skip, but the description says only a required stage needs a written reason. Optional stages could previously be skipped without one, so this changes how they behave. The precondition's own reason text also says a stage is skipped only with a reason. The description and the check disagree about whether optional stages need a reason.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.skip_stage: description vs precondition skip-needs-a-reason", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.1.1. {"problem": "The check only rejects an empty string. A whitespace-only reason passes, so the owner can skip a required stage without actually writing why, which defeats the purpose of the change.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.skip_stage, precondition skip-needs-a-reason", "kind": "other"}
A.1.2. {"problem": "The declaration has no notion of which stages are required or optional. The rule is enforced only by the kernel's `skip_stage` call, which was designed to skip optional stages only. Nothing in the change shows the kernel now accepts a required stage with a reason. If it still refuses, the owner's skip passes the precondition and then fails when deployed. The change cannot work without a matching kernel change.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.skip_stage effects (call: skip_stage) and record_skip", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.1.3. {"problem": "The reason for skipping a required stage is not stored anywhere in the domain. `record_skip` has no effects and takes only `skipped: boolean`, and Draft has no field for skipped stages or reasons. The justification the change requires is therefore not recorded or auditable in the declaration.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.record_skip and objects.Draft", "kind": "other"}
A.1.4. {"problem": "The domain's behaviour and interface changed, but the domain `version` is still 14 and the composition still pins interface 1. No scenario covers `skip_stage`, either the denial by `skip-needs-a-reason` or a successful skip with a reason, so the new rule is untested.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: version and scenarios", "kind": "other"}
A.2.0. {"problem": "The description says only a required stage needs a written reason, but the new precondition `params.reason != ''` applies to every skip. Skipping an optional stage, which needed no reason before, is now refused without one. The old behaviour for optional stages changes without being stated. Also, `reason` was already a required string param, so the check only rejects an empty string. A whitespace-only reason passes.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.skip_stage, description and precondition skip-needs-a-reason", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.2.1. {"problem": "Nothing in the declaration distinguishes required from optional stages. The stage is a free string, and the decision on whether it may be skipped sits in the kernel's `skip_stage` call. The change declares that required stages can be skipped, but the kernel that serves the stage bundles may still refuse to skip a required stage. As a result the declared rule and the deployed behaviour can diverge.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.skip_stage effects (call: skip_stage) and record_skip", "kind": "other"}
A.2.2. {"problem": "The reason is only passed to the kernel call. `record_skip` has no effects and Draft has no property for skipped stages or reasons. The written justification for skipping a required stage is therefore not kept in the draft state, and `record_skip` only takes a `skipped` boolean. The 'if they write why' intent is not auditable.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.record_skip and objects.Draft", "kind": "other"}
A.2.3. {"problem": "The change alters the behaviour of an action marked `status: confirmed`, sourced to ADR-0037. The new precondition is only `proposed` and has no source. The confirmed action's description now states a rule no person has confirmed. The action also has no scenario covering a skip with and without a reason, even though a Draft fixture D1 exists. The domain `version: 14` is not changed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.skip_stage status/source, scenarios, version", "kind": "other"}
A.3.0. {"problem": "The description says only a required stage needs a written reason. The new precondition `params.reason != ''` applies to every skip, optional stages included. Skipping an optional stage with an empty reason was allowed before and is now denied, which is a behaviour change the request did not ask for. The check also cannot tell required stages from optional ones, so the description and the rule disagree.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action skip_stage: description vs precondition skip-needs-a-reason", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.3.1. {"problem": "The check `params.reason != ''` accepts a whitespace-only reason, so it does not enforce a 'written reason'. Nothing in the declaration shows that the kernel's `skip_stage` call, which previously handled only optional stages, will accept a required stage. The declaration now promises a capability that may still be refused downstream.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action skip_stage: precondition skip-needs-a-reason and effect call skip_stage", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.3.2. {"problem": "The reason is not kept anywhere on the declaration side. `record_skip` takes only `skipped: boolean` and sets nothing, and Draft has no property for skipped stages or reasons. A required stage can now be skipped, but the interview state does not record that it was skipped or why. So the required-stage check and the audit trail depend on something the domain does not model.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action record_skip and object Draft", "kind": "other"}
A.3.3. {"problem": "The new rule has no scenario. Nothing tests that a skip with an empty reason is denied by skip-needs-a-reason, or that a required stage can be skipped with a reason. The existing scenarios do not cover skip_stage at all.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenarios", "kind": "other"}
A.3.4. {"problem": "The action stays `status: confirmed` and keeps its ADR-0037 record source, although its meaning changed (it now covers required stages). The new precondition is only `proposed` and has no source or owner confirmation. A confirmed action therefore carries unconfirmed behaviour, and its source does not support the new behaviour.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action skip_stage: status/source vs precondition skip-needs-a-reason (proposed)", "kind": "other"}
A.4.0. {"problem": "The new precondition `params.reason != ''` runs for every skip, not only for required stages. Skipping an optional stage used to need no reason, and now an empty reason is denied. The description says only a required stage needs a written reason, and the change request asks only to allow skipping required stages. The rule never looks at the stage, so it cannot tell required from optional. It also accepts a whitespace-only reason.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action skip_stage, precondition skip-needs-a-reason", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.4.1. {"problem": "The written reason is not kept anywhere in the domain. The reason is only passed to the kernel call. `record_skip` has no effects, and its `skipped` param is not stored. Draft has no property for skipped stages or reasons. So a required stage can be skipped with no durable record of why, and the reason cannot be checked or shown later. This defeats the purpose of requiring it.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions record_skip and skip_stage, object Draft", "kind": "other"}
A.4.2. {"problem": "The declaration alone may not allow skipping a required stage. `skip_stage` delegates to the kernel `skip_stage` call, which presumably enforces which stages are required. Nothing in the change shows that the kernel call accepts a required stage with a reason. If it still refuses, the new description is false and the owner is still blocked.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action skip_stage, effect call skip_stage", "kind": "other"}
A.4.3. {"problem": "No scenario covers `skip_stage`, either the new precondition denying an empty reason or the allowed skip of a required stage. The new rule is also `proposed` on an action that is `confirmed`. The domain's `version` and the interface pinned by the composition were not changed for this behaviour change.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenarios, skip_stage precondition and version", "kind": "other"}
A.5.0. {"problem": "The new precondition `params.reason != ''` applies to every skip, optional stages included, while the description says only a required stage needs a written reason. The check cannot tell required from optional, so optional skips now also need a reason. The description and the rule disagree. An all-whitespace reason also passes.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml, action skip_stage, precondition skip-needs-a-reason and its description", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.5.1. {"problem": "The declaration now allows skipping a required stage, but the effect still calls the kernel `skip_stage` call. That call is what treated required stages as non-skippable, and nothing here shows it accepts them. Once deployed, a required-stage skip may be refused by the kernel. `record_skip` only takes a `skipped` boolean and sets nothing, so the skip and its reason are never recorded on the Draft. Nothing then shows a required stage was skipped, and `current_stage` and the bundle flow do not change.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml, actions skip_stage and record_skip, object Draft", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.5.2. {"problem": "The skip_stage action stays `status: confirmed`, with its source an old ADR record, even though its meaning has changed from optional-only to optional-or-required. The owner never confirmed the new behaviour. The only new element, the precondition, is `proposed`, so the confirmed description claims a rule that is not confirmed. The required-stage skip needs a source and a confirmation.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml, action skip_stage (description, source, status)", "kind": "other"}
A.5.3. {"problem": "No scenario exercises the new rule: no skip with an empty reason being denied by skip-needs-a-reason, and no skip of a required stage. The Draft transition matrix entry for skip_stage still cites the old source and says nothing about required stages. The rule is untested.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml, scenarios and transitions.Draft.open.skip_stage", "kind": "other"}
A.5.4. {"problem": "The domain's behaviour changes: a skip that used to be refused is now allowed, and a new precondition is added. The domain `version` stays at 14. The composition pins authoring `interface: 1`, so consumers get no signal of the change.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml, version; toprope-self.composition.yaml, members.authoring", "kind": "behaviour"}

